answer:
## The one-sentence definition

React is a **declarative, component-based JavaScript library for building user interfaces**, maintained by Meta.

---

## Unpacking that

| Word | What it means in practice |
|------|---------------------------|
| **Declarative** | You describe the UI for a given state. You never write `document.createElement` or manually patch the DOM. |
| **Component-based** | The UI is a tree of reusable functions that each return a piece of UI. |
| **Library** | It solves rendering. Routing, data fetching, forms and styling come from elsewhere. |

---

## Imperative vs declarative

```jsx
// Imperative (vanilla DOM) — you list every step
const el = document.createElement('p');
el.textContent = `Count: ${count}`;
el.className = 'counter';
document.body.appendChild(el);
// ...and remember to update it later when count changes

// Declarative (React) — you state the result
function Counter({ count }) {
  return <p className="counter">Count: {count}</p>;
}
```

When `count` changes, React re-runs the function, compares the result to the previous one, and applies the minimum set of DOM changes.

---

## Why interviewers ask this

They want to hear **"library"** and **"declarative"**. Saying "React is a framework" is the single most common way to open badly. The follow-up is usually "so what does React *not* give you?" — answer: routing, HTTP, global state, styling, build tooling.

---

## Where it runs

React itself is renderer-agnostic. `react-dom` renders to the browser, `react-native` to native mobile views, and other renderers exist for canvas, terminals and PDFs. That split is why the package is `react` plus a separate renderer.

## The problem

Comparing two arbitrary trees optimally is **O(n³)**. For a thousand nodes that is a billion operations — unusable. React drops to **O(n)** by assuming two things that are almost always true in real UIs.

---

## Heuristic 1 — different type, different tree

If the element type at a position changes, React does not try to diff further. It unmounts the old subtree entirely (losing its state) and mounts the new one.

```jsx
// <Counter /> unmounts completely and remounts — its state resets to 0
{isEditing ? <div><Counter /></div> : <span><Counter /></span>}
```

This is also the classic cause of "my input keeps losing focus" and "my state mysteriously resets":

```jsx
// ❌ A new component type is created on every render, so React
// unmounts and remounts the whole subtree every single time
function Parent() {
  function Child() { return <input />; }   // new function identity each render
  return <Child />;
}

// ✅ Define it outside, so the type is stable across renders
function Child() { return <input />; }
function Parent() { return <Child />; }
```

---

## Heuristic 2 — keys identify siblings

Within a list, React matches children across renders by `key` rather than by position. That is the entire reason keys exist.

```jsx
// Without keys, inserting at the front makes React think every item changed
{users.map((user) => <Row key={user.id} user={user} />)}
```

---

## Same type, same position

When the type matches, React keeps the DOM node and the component instance, updates only the props that differ, and recurses into the children.

---

## Fiber, briefly

Since React 16 the reconciler is **Fiber**, which splits rendering into interruptible units of work. That is what makes concurrent features possible — React can start rendering, pause to handle a keystroke, and resume. The diffing heuristics above did not change; the scheduling around them did.

## The contrast

**Imperative:** "Turn left, go 400m, turn right."
**Declarative:** "Take me to 12 Oak Street."

Both get you there. Only one still works when the road is closed.

---

## In code

```js
// Imperative — every transition is your problem
function setLoading(isLoading) {
  if (isLoading) {
    spinner.style.display = 'block';
    button.disabled = true;
    errorBox.style.display = 'none';
  } else {
    spinner.style.display = 'none';
    button.disabled = false;
  }
}
```

```jsx
// Declarative — one description, all transitions derived
function SaveButton({ status }) {
  return (
    <>
      {status === 'loading' && <Spinner />}
      {status === 'error' && <ErrorBox />}
      <button disabled={status === 'loading'}>Save</button>
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## Why this matters as your app grows

With N states, imperative code has up to **N × N** transitions to handle, and forgetting one produces the classic bug where the spinner stays on screen after an error. Declarative code has **N** descriptions and React derives the transitions.

> The mental shift interviewers listen for: **UI is a function of state**, often written `UI = f(state)`. Change the state, and the correct UI follows automatically.

---

## Where React is still imperative

Refs. When you need to focus an input, play a video or measure a DOM node, you reach through the declarative layer with `useRef` and touch the node directly. It is a deliberate escape hatch, not a failure of the model.

## The rule

**Data goes down through props. Changes go up through callbacks.**

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');

  //          data down ↓        change up ↑
  return <Child value={name} onChange={setName} />;
}

function Child({ value, onChange }) {
  // The child cannot reassign the parent's state.
  // It can only ask the parent to change it.
  return <input value={value} onChange={(e) => onChange(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

---

## Why one-way instead of two-way

With two-way binding (Angular's `ngModel`, Vue's `v-model`), a child can write straight back into the parent's data. Convenient for forms, painful to debug at scale: when a value is wrong, any component holding a reference could be responsible.

One-way binding makes it a search problem with one answer. The value came from a prop. Follow the prop up. Repeat until you find the `useState` that owns it.

| | One-way (React) | Two-way (Angular, Vue) |
|---|---|---|
| Data flow | Parent → child only | Both directions |
| Form boilerplate | More | Less |
| Debugging | Trace one path | Trace many writers |
| Predictability | High | Lower at scale |

---

## The common objection

"But `v-model` is so much less typing for forms." True. React's answer is that form binding is a small, local cost, and libraries like React Hook Form remove most of it. The traceability is worth it once an app has hundreds of components.

---

## Related concept

When two siblings need the same data, you **lift state up** to their closest common parent and pass it down to both. That is one-way data flow forcing you toward a single source of truth.

## The three levels

```jsx
// COMPONENT — the function. A blueprint.
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>;
}

// ELEMENT — a plain object describing a desired render.
// This does NOT call Greeting. It just records the intent.
const element = <Greeting name="Ada" />;

console.log(element);
// { type: Greeting, props: { name: 'Ada' }, key: null, ... }

// INSTANCE — created by React when it mounts the element.
// It holds the state and hooks. You never construct it yourself.
root.render(element);
```

---

## Why the distinction matters

**Elements are immutable and cheap.** Once created you cannot change one — you create a new element to describe the next state. Creating a thousand elements costs nothing; they are just objects.

**Creating an element does not render it.** This trips people up:

```jsx
// This does not run ExpensiveComponent's body.
// It just builds { type: ExpensiveComponent, props: {} }.
const el = <ExpensiveComponent />;

// It only renders when React commits it to the tree.
```

That is exactly why passing children as a prop can skip re-renders — the parent creates the element object, but if the element is the same reference, React can bail out of re-rendering the subtree.

---

## Summary table

| | What it is | Who creates it | Mutable |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Component** | Function returning elements | You | n/a |
| **Element** | Object describing desired UI | You, via JSX | No |
| **Instance** | Mounted component with state | React | Internal |

---

## The interview trap

"Is `<Greeting />` a component?" — No. `Greeting` is the component; `<Greeting />` is an **element** that describes rendering it.

## What hydration does

```text
Server:  renders HTML  →  sends real markup  →  user sees content (not interactive)
Client:  downloads JS  →  renders the same tree  →  attaches handlers to existing DOM
```

The key point: hydration **reuses** the existing DOM nodes. It does not recreate them. That is what makes it faster than a full client render and why the page does not flash.

---

## What causes a mismatch

Anything that renders differently in the two environments:

```jsx
// ❌ Server time ≠ client time
function Clock() {
  return <p>{new Date().toLocaleTimeString()}</p>;
}

// ❌ Different random value in each environment
<div id={`item-${Math.random()}`} />

// ❌ `window` does not exist on the server
const isMobile = window.innerWidth < 768;

// ❌ localStorage is browser-only
const theme = localStorage.getItem('theme');
```

Invalid HTML nesting causes it too — the browser silently repairs `<p><div/></p>` into something React did not render, and the trees no longer match.

---

## The fixes

**Render the browser-only value after mount:**

```jsx
function Clock() {
  const [time, setTime] = useState(null);      // matches server: null
  useEffect(() => setTime(new Date()), []);    // fills in on the client
  return <p>{time ? time.toLocaleTimeString() : 'Loading…'}</p>;
}
```

**Or opt a single value out explicitly:**

```jsx
// React will not warn about this element's text content
<time suppressHydrationWarning>{new Date().toISOString()}</time>
```

`suppressHydrationWarning` only applies one level deep and only silences the warning — use it for genuinely unavoidable differences like timestamps, not to paper over a real bug.

---

## Why it is worth taking seriously

A mismatch is not cosmetic. React discards the server HTML for that subtree and re-renders on the client, which throws away the performance benefit you paid for by rendering on the server in the first place.

## What it looks like

```jsx
import { StrictMode } from 'react';

createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <StrictMode>
    <App />
  </StrictMode>
);
```

It renders no DOM and has zero production cost — the behaviour is development-only.

---

## What gets double-invoked

- Component function bodies
- `useState`, `useMemo` and `useReducer` initialiser functions
- State updater functions
- Effects: mount → cleanup → mount again

---

## Why this is a feature, not a bug

**Double rendering catches impurity.** A component must be a pure function of its props and state. Running it twice makes side effects during render visible immediately:

```jsx
let total = 0;

function Cart({ items }) {
  total += items.length;    // ❌ mutating outside state during render
  return <p>{total}</p>;    // Doubles under StrictMode — bug exposed
}
```

**Double-mounting effects catches missing cleanup.** Mount, unmount, mount again simulates what actually happens when a user navigates away and back:

```jsx
// ❌ Every mount adds a listener; none are ever removed
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
}, []);

// ✅ Cleanup makes remounting safe
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
}, []);
```

---

## The mistake people make

Seeing an API call fire twice in development and "fixing" it with a ref guard:

```jsx
// ❌ Hiding the symptom
const didFetch = useRef(false);
useEffect(() => {
  if (didFetch.current) return;
  didFetch.current = true;
  fetchData();
}, []);
```

The double call is telling you the effect is not resilient to remounting. The real fix is an `AbortController` in the cleanup, or a data library like React Query that deduplicates requests.

> Removing `<StrictMode>` to stop the double render is trading a loud development warning for a silent production bug.

## The contract

A component's render is a **calculation**. Calculations do not do things — they return values.

```jsx
// ✅ Pure — depends only on props, changes nothing
function Invoice({ items, taxRate }) {
  const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
  return <p>Total: {subtotal * (1 + taxRate)}</p>;
}
```

---

## What breaks purity

```jsx
let renderCount = 0;

function Broken({ items, user }) {
  renderCount++;                    // ❌ mutating an outer variable
  items.sort((a, b) => a.id - b.id); // ❌ mutating a prop in place
  user.lastSeen = Date.now();        // ❌ mutating a prop object
  document.title = 'Invoice';        // ❌ touching the DOM during render
  analytics.track('viewed');         // ❌ side effect during render
  return <List items={items} />;
}
```

```jsx
// ✅ Fixed — copy before sorting, move effects out
function Fixed({ items }) {
  const sorted = [...items].sort((a, b) => a.id - b.id);  // or items.toSorted()

  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = 'Invoice';
    analytics.track('viewed');
  }, []);

  return <List items={sorted} />;
}
```

---

## Where side effects are allowed

| Where | When it runs | Use for |
|-------|--------------|---------|
| **Event handlers** | On user interaction | Submitting, navigating, most things |
| **`useEffect`** | After render commits | Syncing with systems outside React |
| **Render body** | During render | Nothing with side effects |

Prefer event handlers. Most code people put in effects belongs in the handler that caused it.

---

## Why React insists on this

Purity is what makes React's features safe:

- **StrictMode** can double-render to find bugs
- **Concurrent rendering** can start, abandon and restart a render
- **`memo`** can skip a render entirely and be sure the output would have matched
- **Server Components** can render once and cache the result

Every one of those breaks if rendering has side effects. This is why purity comes up in senior interviews — it is the assumption the whole modern architecture rests on.

## The two phases

| | Render phase | Commit phase |
|---|---|---|
| **What happens** | Call components, build tree, diff | Mutate the DOM, run effects |
| **Interruptible** | Yes | No |
| **Can be discarded** | Yes | No |
| **Must be pure** | Yes | Side effects expected |
| **Runs** | Possibly several times | Once per commit |

---

## Why the split exists

Because the render phase is pure and interruptible, React can begin rendering a low-priority update, notice the user typed, **abandon that work entirely**, handle the keystroke, and start again.

That is only safe if rendering had no side effects. If your component had already fired an API call or mutated a global, abandoning the render would leave the app inconsistent. This is the concrete reason purity is enforced.

---

## Where your code runs

```jsx
function Profile({ userId }) {
  // RENDER PHASE — may run multiple times, may be discarded
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const initials = getInitials(user?.name);

  // COMMIT PHASE — runs after the DOM is updated
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = user?.name ?? 'Loading';
  }, [user]);

  // COMMIT PHASE, before the browser paints
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const { height } = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect();
    setHeight(height);
  }, []);

  return <h1>{initials}</h1>;   // ← the render phase's return value
}
```

---

## Commit is split in two

1. **Mutation** — React applies DOM changes. `useLayoutEffect` runs here, synchronously, before the browser paints. That is why you use it for measurements that would otherwise cause a visible flicker, and why blocking work in it is costly.
2. **Passive effects** — `useEffect` runs after paint, so it does not delay what the user sees.

> The practical rule that falls out of this: **measure or synchronously adjust layout in `useLayoutEffect`; do everything else in `useEffect`.**
